      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first permanent settlers were Hispanic New Mexican families in the late 1800s, including the Baca, Atencio, and Romero clans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concho%2C_Arizona",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Don Manuel Antonio Candelaria, a former Apache captive, is credited as Concho’s first known settler, arriving in the 1840s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concho%2C_Arizona",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Concho grew from a Spanish‑era ranching outpost that later attracted Mormon settlers in the late 19th century, turning it into an agricultural hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://finenaturalist.blog/concho-az-apache-county-guide",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Every year, Concho hosts the San Rafael Fiestas, gathering residents and relatives from afar for music, food, and storytelling.",
